  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 106 AM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nAs we head into the weekend and into early next week, a multi hazard \nevent will be taking shape as an upper level low lingers in the \nregion and strong surface high pressure builds into the eastern US. \nEnhanced moisture around the upper low will bring rounds of showers, \nheavy at times, to SE FL from Saturday through Monday. While mean \nQPF values have trended downward the past several days, 1-3 inches \nis still expected across the east coast metro, with localized higher \nend amounts of 4-6 inches possible. This rain combined with the \nhigher king tides this weekend may present some flooding issues. WPC \nhas a Slight risk of excessive rain across the metro both days this \nweekend. The other main story will be the gusty winds this weekend \nas the pressure gradient tightens. NE winds will gust 25-35 mph on \nSaturday and 30-40 mph on Sunday. \n\nConditions begin to improve Tuesday into mid week as the upper low \npulls away from the region to the northeast and with it the deeper \nmoisture will exit the area. There will still be scattered coastal \nshowers around both days, but the rainfall flooding threat will \ndecrease. \n\nFairly comfortable temperatures are expected this weekend into next \nweek with high temps generally in the low to middle 80s, and low \ntemps each day in the 60s/70s.",
